 `enable-subrole laptop` adds `configure-laptop` (power-profiles-daemon).

+`[server]` includes `@subrole.hyperv-guest`. Another role can use that
+same line, or `enable-subrole hyperv-guest`. The module returns
+immediately unless `systemd-detect-virt` prints `microsoft`, so a
+bare-metal server is unchanged.
+
+On a Hyper-V guest it installs `hyperv-tools`. OpenMandriva publishes
+the KVP, VSS, and file-copy guest services under that name (Rock
+`6.14.2-3` and cooker `7.2.9-1`); there is no `hyperv-daemons` package.
+The units are `hypervkvpd.service`, `hypervvssd.service`, and
+`hypervfcopyd.service`. They still `ExecStart` the Fedora paths
+`/usr/sbin/hypervkvpd`, `hypervvssd`, and `hypervfcopyd`, which this
+package does not install. The binaries on disk are
+`/usr/bin/hv_kvp_daemon`, `hv_vss_daemon`, and `hv_fcopy_uio_daemon`,
+so the module adds an `ExecStart` drop-in for each. The units have no
+`[Install]` section. Udev starts them when the vmbus devices appear,
+and the module starts them on the run that installs them.
+
+`chrony` comes from the extra repo and `chronyd.service` is enabled.
+Hyper-V time sync stays in `hv_utils`. That module also publishes the
+KVP, VSS, and file-copy devices, so it is not blacklisted, and the
+host integration service stays on. `chronyd` is NTP while the guest is
+up; `hv_utils` still steps the clock at boot and after resume. If the
+two fight, turn off Time synchronization on the VM in Hyper-V.
+
+`/etc/dracut.conf.d/hyperv-guest.conf` uses `force_drivers` for
+`hv_vmbus`, `hv_storvsc`, `hv_netvsc`, and `hv_utils` (the module names
+in the Rock znver1 `kernel-server` package). `dracut -f` runs only when
+that file changes. A failed rebuild restores the previous file so the
+next run tries again.
+
